{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"1484 IMPLEMENTED (both seams), awaiting AC5 evidence. The ticket has FIVE ACs, not one —\nit also requires orchestrator.py under 700. Both splits are done, lint+mypy clean, oracle green:\n\n  gate_dispatch.py            799 -> 527   (271 headroom)  + workflow/plan_review_recovery.py  323 new\n  plan_review/orchestrator.py 796 -> 646   (154 headroom)  + plan_review/context_assembly.py   194 new\n\nZERO existing test edits (git status: 2 modified src, 2 new src, 1 new test). The re-import\nmechanism held exactly as in 2682/3a98.\n\nTHREE things worth carrying forward:\n\n1. ruff --fix would have BROKEN the build silently. After seam 2, ruff flagged 8 unused imports in\n   orchestrator. Seven were dead, but `largest_window_tokens` has FOUR test call sites reaching it\n   as `orchestrator.largest_window_tokens`. It is now kept with an explicit noqa + comment saying\n   why. Any future \"tidy the unused imports\" pass on orchestrator.py must re-check this.\n   Same class of trap as the flattened-import caution already on this ticket.\n\n2. RED was proven under revert-first, and one new test legitimately had NO red state.\n   Reverting seam 2 gave 5 failed / 7 passed with the right reasons (incl. \"orchestrator.py is 796\n   LOC; the split must bring it under 700\"). But\n   test_moved_assembly_names_are_still_reachable_from_orchestrator PASSED while reverted — correctly,\n   since pre-split orchestrator defines those names natively. It is a FORWARD regression guard, not a\n   RED-first test. Teeth proven by mutation instead: dropping the largest_window_tokens re-export\n   failed exactly that test; making assemble_context bypass the memo failed exactly the memo test.\n   Both mutations were asserted-as-applied BEFORE the red check (silent no-op mutations produced a\n   false \"has teeth\" result three times earlier in this run).\n\n3. AC5 needs TWO pytest summary lines (pre-split + post-split). Getting the pre-split one required a\n   separate detached worktree at HEAD 96d683a0d with its OWN venv — verified it resolves rebar to\n   /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-wt-1484-baseline/src, not the split tree, or the measurement would be a\n   lie. Both suites now run IN PARALLEL in different trees (never two workers in one tree).\n   Post-seam-1 solo run was already green: 8153 passed, 74 skipped, 0 failed in 26:09.\n\nNOT extracted, deliberately: the code-review gate cluster. code_review_enabled is monkeypatched BY\nSTRING on the gate_dispatch module object at 14 sites; moving its sole internal caller with it would\nmake all 14 patches silently stop taking effect. That needs a reference-style change = a design\nchange, which must not ride inside a size-driven relocation. Separate ticket.\n\nGerrit: stack TOP (1090) is LLM-Review +1 / Verified +1. The five Verified -1 (1014, 1023, 1043,\n1048, 1067) are the known mid-stack module-size transients the operator overrides.\n\nCRITICAL PATH CONFIRMED BY THE GATE ITSELF: f844 and a574 both hold certified, current attestations\n(review-plan --status, read-only, no LLM). eb58 is UNSIGNED and is blocked by 1484, so its review\nwould fast-fail until 1484 closes. Order is 1484 -> eb58 -> f844 -> cb6f/172e/7761/cc33/d23e."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785477876000435001,"uuid":"701596da-7f58-427f-b41a-ba9922c28cdd"}